  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1133 AM MDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nAn upper low and the tropical remnants of Rachel have combined today \nand will bring some showers and thunderstorms to the region today \nand tonight. Will start to see more showers and storms developing \ninitially in the Gila region early this afternoon and storms will\ngradually trek eastward into the evening hours. Much of the storm\nactivity will be east of the Rio Grande by around 9PM this \nevening. Some lingering showers possible during the overnight \nhours. In addition, we will see breezy conditions across the area \nwith sustained winds of 15-25 mph out of the southwest with gusts \nto 40 mph. Winds will relax after sunset tonight. Continued \nbreeziness Monday and Tuesday afternoons but won't be as breezy as\nwhat we'll see today. \n\nBy Monday, we're still going to be sandwiched in between an upper\nlow to our northwest and a ridge to our southeast over the Gulf. \nThe area will be in mostly dry southwest flow but some moisture \nbeing dragged up from the south (partially from remnants of Simon \nbut also some Gulf moisture) which will give south and east \nportions of Hudspeth and Otero counties a chance for showers and \nthunderstorms tomorrow evening, mostly after 6PM which could \nlinger for a while after dark. Tuesday and Wednesday will feature \nmostly dry to dry conditions across the area with some breeziness.\nDisagreements between the models start at around Thursday. Long \nrange models still suggest some lingering low pressure to our west\npinching off from the main upper low with ridging to our east. \nModels seem more consistent about a strong backdoor front pushing \ninto the area late Thursday into early Friday morning but still is\nuncertain as to if we will see any rain out of this front. More \nthan likely will need a wind advisory focusing on the west facing \nslopes late Thu-early Fri.",
